---
import DisclosureIcon from "../DisclosureIcon.astro";
import ChapterFrame from "../ChapterFrame.astro";
import PolicyComparison from "../PolicyComparison.astro";
import QuestionSequence from "../renewal/QuestionSequence.astro";
import { discoveryOptions } from "../../data/scenarios.js";
---

<ChapterFrame id="discovery">
  <Fragment slot="heading"
    ><h2 id="discovery-heading">
      The answer is here.<br /><em>What have we learned?</em>
    </h2><p>
      A verified result can be an achievement while leaving its ideas difficult
      to explain, teach, or extend. What should its authors help others
      understand?
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <ol class="discovery-stages" aria-label="Three distinct contributions">
    <li>
      <h3>Find a result.</h3><p>Produce a claim and an argument.</p>
    </li><li>
      <h3>Check the proof.</h3><p>
        Establish what follows from stated assumptions.
      </p>
    </li><li>
      <h3>Share the ideas.</h3><p>
        Explain why it works and what others can build on.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <QuestionSequence kind="discovery"
    ><article data-question>
      <div class="scenario-question">
        <h3>
          A proof is checkable.<br />Its ideas remain difficult to use.
        </h3><p>
          In this illustrative situation, a lab has a formally verified
          mathematical result. How much responsibility should accompany its
          claim to a breakthrough? The comparison concerns how work is shared,
          not a judgement on any particular proof.
        </p>
      </div>
      <PolicyComparison
        id="discovery-publication"
        legend="What should accompany the result?"
        options={discoveryOptions}
      />
    </article></QuestionSequence
  >
  <details class="evidence">
    <summary
      ><span>Navier–Stokes & the purpose of a proof</span><span
        class="summary-hint">Two sources, different claims</span
      ><DisclosureIcon /></summary
    ><div class="evidence-content">
      <div>
        <h3>A claimed solution.</h3><p>
          On 8 September 2026, OpenAI published a claimed Navier–Stokes solution
          and a Lean formalization. Publishing a formalization is not the same
          as this site independently checking the theorem, its assumptions, or
          its correspondence to the prize problem.
        </p><p>
          <a
            href="https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Read OpenAI’s paper announcement ↗</a
          >
        </p><p>
          The company also discusses concurrent work and possible indirect
          contributions from de-identified product-use data. Its account does
          not establish improper use of another researcher’s work. Correctness,
          provenance, permission, and credit remain distinct questions.
        </p>
      </div><div>
        <h3>Understanding is the purpose.</h3><p>
          In a 7 September post on related Alpöge–Buckmaster work, Terence Tao
          emphasises developing mathematical understanding and insight,
          describing problem-solving as a proxy for that goal. He highlights the
          effort to make AI-assisted arguments readable.
        </p><p>
          <a
            href="https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/09/07/finite-time-blowup-with-smooth-forcing-term-for-the-incompressible-porous-medium-boussinesq-and-incompressible-euler-equations/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Tao’s original post ↗</a
          >
        </p><p>
          That post predates OpenAI’s announcement. It is not a verdict on
          OpenAI’s proof. Our inference is that an accountable research claim
          should include a plan for explanation, scrutiny, and continued
          stewardship.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p class="scenario-note">
    Understanding is not a requirement that every reader personally reconstruct
    every proof. The practical question is whether a community has the
    materials, expertise, and support to inspect and develop it. These are
    proposed responsibilities, not a certification of scientific merit.
  </p>
  <div class="chapter-takeaway">
    <p>
      A breakthrough should leave<br /><em>others able to build.</em>
    </p><a href="#obligation-1">01 / Trace contributions and provenance ↗</a><br
    /><a href="#obligation-2">02 / Make evidence usable ↗</a>
  </div>
</ChapterFrame>
